build.reason:
removeAgedItems(boolean) can carry the recipe. Only the gold decides whether anything is aged and whether a SeriesChangeEvent is sent. The omission of the nothing-aged exit is a quiet special-case decision that a caller cannot undo, because an event once sent cannot be withdrawn.

The rule that the event is sent only when items are removed is pinned outside the gold:
- the facade's kept javadoc ("IF any items are removed");
- Series.fireSeriesChanged ("the series has been changed");
- the sibling overload removeAgedItems(long, boolean), which uses `removed && notify`;
- TimeSeries.delete(RegularTimePeriod) and TimeSeries.clear.
Ownership tests: testRemoveAgedItems2 and testDelete2 (both pass on the variant).

The other two candidates were rejected:
- addOrUpdate: each step its three tests check is a mutation that a hop holding the series can redo in one line after the gold returns, so there is no single owner.
- delete(int,int): no one-line omission is possible without a frozen loop or an unused parameter.

The C10i recipe was applied to TimeSeries.removeAgedItems(boolean). The per-item age test, the block removal and the change notification sit in the gold. The original condition (more than one item) and the reference period sit in hops 1 and 2. The convention is pinned outside the gold:
- the intact sibling overload removeAgedItems(long, boolean) has `if (removed && notify)`;
- delete(RegularTimePeriod) fires only when the period is found;
- clear() fires only when the series is not empty;
- the javadoc of Series.fireSeriesChanged says it signals "that the series has been changed";
- the facade's own @param notify says the event is sent IF any items are removed.

Ownership tests: testRemoveAgedItems2, testDelete2, testAddOrUpdate and testSetMaximumItemCount pass on both the correct configuration and the variant. The correct configuration behaves identically to the original: the same leading prefix is removed, and the event is sent exactly when something was removed and notify is true.
